Here's some Java code that should accomplish what you're looking for:
```java
public class LucasSequence {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int limit = 1000;
        long sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < limit; i++) {
            long term = lucas(i);
            if (isPrime(term)) {
                sum += term;
            }
            if (isPalindromicPrime(i)) {
                System.out.println("Index " + i + " is a palindromic prime, term is " + term);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("The sum of all prime terms in the first " + limit + " terms of the Lucas sequence is " + sum);
    }
    public static long lucas(int n) {
        if (n == 0) {
            return 2;
        } else if (n == 1) {
            return 1;
        } else {
            return lucas(n-1) + lucas(n-2);
        }
    }
    public static boolean isPrime(long n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (long i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
    public static boolean isPalindromicPrime(int n) {
        if (isPrime(n)) {
            String str = Integer.toString(n);
            for (int i = 0; i < str.length() / 2; i++) {
                if (str.charAt(i) != str.charAt(str.length() - i - 1)) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```
This program uses a recursive function `lucas` to compute the `n`th term of the Lucas sequence. It then checks whether each term is prime using the `isPrime` function, and adds it to a running total `sum` if it is. At the same time, it checks whether the index of each term is a palindromic prime using the `isPalindromicPrime` function, and prints out the index and term if it is. Finally, it prints out the sum of all prime terms in the first 1000 terms of the Lucas sequence.

