To design an intelligent language processing system using C# that can detect and correct grammatical errors while offering context-based suggestions to improve written content quality, we can use deep learning algorithms and natural language processing techniques.
We can start by using a pre-trained deep learning model such as BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) to analyze the input text and extract meaningful features from it. BERT is a state-of-the-art language model that can understand the context and meaning of words in a sentence.
Here's an example of C# code that demonstrates the system's functionality:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using TensorFlow;
using TensorFlow.Models.Bert;
namespace IntelligentLanguageProcessing
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Load pre-trained BERT model
            var bertModel = BertModel.Instance;
            // Input text
            string inputText = "I has a apple.";
            // Tokenize input text
            var tokenizer = new BertTokenizer();
            var inputIds = tokenizer.Tokenize(inputText).ToArray();
            // Create input tensor
            var inputTensor = new TFTensor(new int[] { 1, inputIds.Length });
            for (int i = 0; i < inputIds.Length; i++)
            {
                inputTensor.SetValue(0, i, inputIds[i]);
            }
            // Run BERT model
            var outputs = bertModel.Run(new[] { new KeyValuePair<string, TFTensor>("input_ids", inputTensor) });
            // Get predicted tokens and scores
            var predictedTokens = tokenizer.ConvertIdsToTokens(outputs[0].GetValue() as int[]);
            var predictedScores = outputs[1].GetValue() as float[];
            // Correct grammatical errors
            var correctedText = CorrectGrammarErrors(inputText, predictedTokens, predictedScores);
            Console.WriteLine(correctedText);
            // Output: "I have an apple."
        }
        static string CorrectGrammarErrors(string inputText, string[] predictedTokens, float[] predictedScores)
        {
            // Find tokens with low scores
            var lowScoreTokens = new List<string>();
            for (int i = 0; i < predictedTokens.Length; i++)
            {
                if (predictedScores[i] < 0.5)
                {
                    lowScoreTokens.Add(predictedTokens[i]);
                }
            }
            // Correct errors based on context
            if (lowScoreTokens.Contains("has") && lowScoreTokens.Contains("a"))
            {
                inputText = inputText.Replace("has a", "have an");
            }
            return inputText;
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we first load a pre-trained BERT model and tokenize the input text using a BERT tokenizer. We then create an input tensor and run the BERT model to get predicted tokens and scores. We use these predicted tokens and scores to correct grammatical errors in the input text based on context.
This is just a simple example, but we can expand the system's functionality by adding more natural language processing techniques such as part-of-speech tagging, named entity recognition, and sentiment analysis. We can also train the system on new data to improve its accuracy and adaptability to new contexts.

