The regular expression formula to extract the name being addressed in a sentence using C# would be:
```
^Hey\s+(\w+)
```
This formula matches the beginning of the string (^), followed by the word "Hey", followed by one or more whitespace characters (\s+), followed by one or more word characters (\w+), which is the name being addressed.
Here's a code snippet that uses this regular expression formula to extract the name "John" from the sentence "Hey John, can you come here for a minute?" in C#:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
class Program {
    static void Main(string[] args) {
        string sentence = "Hey John, can you come here for a minute?";
        string pattern = @"^Hey\s+(\w+)";
        Regex regex = new Regex(pattern);
        Match match = regex.Match(sentence);
        if (match.Success) {
            string name = match.Groups[1].Value;
            Console.WriteLine("Name: " + name);
        } else {
            Console.WriteLine("No name found.");
        }
    }
}
```
This code will output "Name: John" because the regular expression formula successfully matched the name "John" in the sentence.

