Yes, it is possible to create a 3D scatter plot using Go programming language. One way to implement this is by using the Go plotting library called "gonum/plot/vg/vg3d". Here is an example code snippet that shows how to create a 3D scatter plot using this library:
```
package main
import (
    "math/rand"
    "gonum.org/v1/plot/vg"
    "gonum.org/v1/plot/vg/draw"
    "gonum.org/v1/plot/vg/vg3d"
)
func main() {
    // Create a new 3D plot with a default perspective.
    plot, err := vg3d.New(plotWidth, plotHeight, "example plot")
    if err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
    // Create some random data for the scatter plot.
    var data []vg3d.Point
    for i := 0; i < numPoints; i++ {
        x := rand.Float64()
        y := rand.Float64()
        z := rand.Float64()
        data = append(data, vg3d.Point{X: x, Y: y, Z: z})
    }
    // Create a scatter plot with the random data.
    scatter, err := vg3d.NewScatter(data)
    if err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
    // Set the color and size of the scatter points.
    scatter.Color = draw.Black
    scatter.Radius = 5
    // Add the scatter plot to the 3D plot.
    plot.Add(scatter)
    // Save the 3D plot to a file.
    if err := plot.Save(10*vg.Centimeter, 10*vg.Centimeter, "scatter.png"); err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
}
```
In this example, the code creates a new 3D plot using the "vg3d.New" function, generates some random data for the scatter plot, creates a scatter plot using the "vg3d.NewScatter" function, sets the color and size of the scatter points, adds the scatter plot to the 3D plot using the "plot.Add" function, and finally saves the 3D plot to a file using the "plot.Save" function.

