Here is a MATLAB code that can create a 3D model of a double helix structure using the given base pair coordinates and twist angles:
```matlab
% Load the data from the Excel table
data = readtable('base_pairs.xlsx');
% Extract the base pair coordinates and twist angles
x = data.X_Coordinate;
y = data.Y_Coordinate;
z = data.Z_Coordinate;
theta = deg2rad(data.Twist_Angle);
% Define the helix parameters
pitch = 3.4; % Angstroms per base pair
radius = 1.5; % Angstroms
% Generate the coordinates for the first strand
num_bp = length(x);
x1 = zeros(num_bp, 1);
y1 = zeros(num_bp, 1);
z1 = zeros(num_bp, 1);
for i = 1:num_bp
    x1(i) = x(i) + radius*cos(theta(i));
    y1(i) = y(i) + radius*sin(theta(i));
    z1(i) = z(i) + pitch*(i-1);
end
% Generate the coordinates for the second strand
x2 = zeros(num_bp, 1);
y2 = zeros(num_bp, 1);
z2 = zeros(num_bp, 1);
for i = 1:num_bp
    x2(i) = x(i) - radius*cos(theta(i));
    y2(i) = y(i) - radius*sin(theta(i));
    z2(i) = z(i) + pitch*(i-1);
end
% Plot the helix structure
figure;
plot3(x1, y1, z1, 'r', 'LineWidth', 2);
hold on;
plot3(x2, y2, z2, 'b', 'LineWidth', 2);
xlabel('X');
ylabel('Y');
zlabel('Z');
title('Double Helix Structure');
legend('Strand 1', 'Strand 2');
grid on;
```
This code reads the base pair coordinates and twist angles from an Excel table, calculates the coordinates for each strand of the helix using the given parameters, and plots the resulting structure in 3D. You can modify the values of `pitch` and `radius` to adjust the shape of the helix, and the code can handle any number of base pairs and twist angles.

