    cout << "Synonyms for " << arms << ":" << endl;
    for (string synonym : armsSynonyms) {
        cout << "- " << synonym << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code defines a `getSynonyms` function that takes a word as input and returns a vector of synonyms obtained from the Datamuse API. The main function then uses this function to retrieve synonyms for the words "pupils", "apprehensive", "lift", and "arms", and prints them to the console. Note that this code requires the `curl` and `rapidjson` libraries to be installed.

Here's an example Java code that uses the Asana Java client library to retrieve the project and task information, and then calculates the percentage completion:
```java
import com.asana.Client;
import com.asana.models.Project;
import com.asana.models.Task;
import java.util.List;
public class AsanaTaskCompletionPercentage {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Initialize Asana client
        Client client = Client.accessToken("ASANA_ACCESS_TOKEN");
        // Get project information
        Project project = client.projects.findById("PROJECT_ID").execute();
        // Get list of tasks in the project
        List<Task> tasks = client.tasks.findByProject(project.id).execute();
        // Calculate percentage completion
        int totalTasks = tasks.size();
        int completedTasks = 0;
        for (Task task : tasks) {
            if (task.completed) {
                completedTasks++;
            }
        }
        double percentageCompletion = ((double) completedTasks / totalTasks) * 100;
        // Display percentage completion to the user
        System.out.println("Project \"" + project.name + "\" is " + percentageCompletion + "% complete.");
    }
}
```
Replace "ASANA_ACCESS_TOKEN" with your actual Asana access token, and "PROJECT_ID" with the ID of the project you want to calculate the percentage completion for. The code retrieves the project information, gets a list of tasks in the project, and then loops through the tasks to count the number of completed tasks. Finally, it calculates the percentage completion and displays it to the user.

Integrating voice recognition technology into a Go mobile app involves several steps, including:
1. Choosing a voice recognition API: Developers can choose from several voice recognition APIs such as Google Cloud Speech-to-Text, Amazon Transcribe, and Microsoft Azure Speech Services.
2. Implementing the voice recognition API: Developers need to integrate the chosen API into their Go code to enable voice recognition in the app.
3. Developing voice commands and natural language processing: Developers can use the API to recognize specific voice commands and process natural language input.
Here is an example of Go code demonstrating the use of dictation, voice commands, and natural language processing using the Google Cloud Speech-to-Text API:
```
// Import the necessary packages
import (
    "cloud.google.com/go/speech/apiv1"
    "context"
    "fmt"
    speechpb "google.golang.org/genproto/googleapis/cloud/speech/v1"
)
// Initialize the speech client
ctx := context.Background()
client, err := speech.NewClient(ctx)
if err != nil {
    log.Fatal(err)
}
// Set the audio configuration
config := &speechpb.RecognitionConfig{
    Encoding:        speechpb.RecognitionConfig_LINEAR16,
    SampleRateHertz: 16000,
    LanguageCode:    "en-US",
}
// Create a new recognition request
req := &speechpb.RecognizeRequest{
    Config: config,
}
// Start the microphone and capture audio
stream, err := client.StreamingRecognize(ctx)
if err != nil {
    log.Fatal(err)
}
// Process the audio stream
for {
    // Read audio data from the microphone
    audioData, err := readAudioData()
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    // Send the audio data to the speech API
    req.Audio = &speechpb.RecognitionAudio{
        AudioSource: &speechpb.RecognitionAudio_Content{Content: audioData},
    }
    err = stream.Send(req)
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    // Receive the speech recognition results
    resp, err := stream.Recv()
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    // Process the speech recognition results
    for _, result := range resp.Results {
        for _, alt := range result.Alternatives {
            fmt.Printf("Transcript: %s\n", alt.Transcript)
        }
    }
}
